1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,915
This video is about ambiguous grammars. A grammar is ambiguous when one input string has two parse trees.

2
00:00:08,365 --> 00:00:31,085
We see why one tree matters. Then we remove ambiguity with precedence and associativity. Then the dangling else problem. At the end we solve exercises from the course notes and the short questions in questions-pre 12.3. The week 5 slides are not available yet, so this video uses the course notes, chapter 4.

3
00:00:31,535 --> 00:00:43,865
A parser reads an input string and uses the grammar to build a parse tree. Here the grammar has E goes to E plus E, or an integer. The input is 1 plus 2 plus 3.

4
00:00:44,315 --> 00:00:48,390
One parse tree groups 1 plus 2 first, and then adds 3.

5
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:56,797
But the same grammar also allows a second tree. It groups 2 plus 3 first. So which tree does the parser give?

6
00:00:57,247 --> 00:01:05,439
The later phases of the compiler use the tree. If one string has two trees, the string can mean two different programs.

7
00:01:05,889 --> 00:01:18,732
Here is the definition from the course notes. A grammar is ambiguous if there is a legal input string with two different derivations. Two different derivations give two different parse trees.

8
00:01:19,182 --> 00:01:23,065
The test: take one input string, and do two derivations.

9
00:01:23,515 --> 00:01:31,109
If both derivations accept, and they are different, the grammar is ambiguous. One example string is a proof.

10
00:01:31,559 --> 00:01:42,247
The hard part is to find that string. In theory this is undecidable. There are grammars for which no program can compute an ambiguity-revealing string.

11
00:01:42,697 --> 00:01:51,700
In practice, a little intuition is enough, for every grammar in this course. Look first at binary operators and at optional parts.

12
00:01:52,150 --> 00:02:02,881
Here is the grammar from the notes. S goes to E. E goes to E plus E, or to an integer. Is it ambiguous? Try the string 1 plus 2 plus 3.

13
00:02:03,331 --> 00:02:08,472
Tree 1. Expand the top E as E plus E. The right E becomes 3.

14
00:02:08,922 --> 00:02:17,839
The left E is again E plus E, and it becomes 1 plus 2. So tree 1 groups the string as 1 plus 2, then plus 3.

15
00:02:18,289 --> 00:02:24,519
Tree 2. Expand the top E as E plus E again. This time the left E becomes 1.

16
00:02:24,969 --> 00:02:33,182
The right E is E plus E, and it becomes 2 plus 3. So tree 2 groups the string as 1 plus, 2 plus 3.

17
00:02:33,632 --> 00:02:49,269
Both trees accept the same string, and they are different. So the grammar is ambiguous. Both trees give 6, because addition is associative. The grammar does not say if plus is left associative or right associative.

18
00:02:49,719 --> 00:02:57,101
Both trees gave the right sum. So why do engineers remove the ambiguity? The notes give two reasons.

19
00:02:57,551 --> 00:03:12,335
Reason 1 is cost. An ambiguous context free grammar is not L L one. Its parser needs a nondeterministic pushdown automaton. An L L one grammar needs only a deterministic one.

20
00:03:12,785 --> 00:03:18,417
The algorithm is more complex: the Earley algorithm instead of recursive descent.

21
00:03:18,867 --> 00:03:23,091
And it takes more time. Order n cubed instead of order n.

22
00:03:23,541 --> 00:03:36,618
Reason 2 is testing. With one acceptable output, testing is easier. Associativity and precedence are the usual sources of ambiguity, and we can design them out of the grammar.

23
00:03:37,068 --> 00:03:45,623
Add multiplication. E goes to E plus E, E times E, or an integer. Take the string 1 plus 2 times 3.

24
00:03:46,073 --> 00:03:51,897
Tree 1 puts plus lower in the tree. It groups 1 plus 2 first, then times 3.

25
00:03:52,347 --> 00:03:57,211
Tree 2 puts times lower in the tree. It groups 2 times 3 first.

26
00:03:57,661 --> 00:04:07,069
Tree 1 gives 9. Tree 2 gives 7. Only 7 is arithmetically correct. The operator lower in the tree is done first.

27
00:04:07,519 --> 00:04:16,521
So now the ambiguity changes the result. The grammar must say that times comes before plus. This is operator precedence.

28
00:04:16,971 --> 00:04:29,473
In school we learn the order of operations, BEDMAS. A grammar must build this in. The notes give four rules. Rule a. Each level of precedence gets its own nonterminal.

29
00:04:29,923 --> 00:04:36,429
Rule b. The lowest level of precedence is the top level production. Here that is E, for plus.

30
00:04:36,879 --> 00:04:43,471
Rule c. Each production uses the next higher level. E uses F, the level for times.

31
00:04:43,921 --> 00:04:48,487
Rule d. The highest level includes the operands, the integers.

32
00:04:48,937 --> 00:04:58,217
The result: low precedence operators are near the root of the tree. High precedence operators are near the leaves, so they are done first.

33
00:04:58,667 --> 00:05:08,928
Now apply the rules to E plus E, E times E. Rule a. We have two operators, so two levels. Call them E for plus, and F for times.

34
00:05:09,378 --> 00:05:16,205
Rule d. F is the highest level, so F includes the operands. Add F goes to an integer.

35
00:05:16,655 --> 00:05:22,244
Rule b. E is the lowest level, so it stays at the top. Keep S goes to E.

36
00:05:22,694 --> 00:05:30,587
Rule c. Connect the levels. E goes to F. Now an E can become an F, but an F can never become a plus.

37
00:05:31,037 --> 00:05:41,576
A python check generated all strings up to length 7. The new grammar accepts exactly the same strings as the old one. Only the trees are different.

38
00:05:42,026 --> 00:05:46,335
Parse 1 plus 2 times 3 with the new grammar. S goes to E.

39
00:05:46,785 --> 00:05:52,012
The plus must come from E goes to E plus E. Only the E level has a plus.

40
00:05:52,462 --> 00:05:59,587
Each side becomes an F. The right F is F times F. The times sits under F, near the leaves.

41
00:06:00,037 --> 00:06:08,464
So the only tree is 1 plus, 2 times 3, which is 7. A python check found exactly one tree for this string.

42
00:06:08,914 --> 00:06:19,666
Is the new grammar unambiguous now? No. Try 4 plus 5 plus 6. The production E goes to E plus E still has an E on both sides.

43
00:06:20,116 --> 00:06:25,364
One tree groups 4 plus 5 first. The other groups 5 plus 6 first.

44
00:06:25,814 --> 00:06:33,089
Both give 15. Addition and multiplication are associative, so arithmetically this does not matter.

45
00:06:33,539 --> 00:06:43,288
Precedence fixes the order between different operators. It does not fix the order when the same operator appears twice. That is associativity.

46
00:06:43,738 --> 00:06:49,541
Change the operators to minus and divide. For these, associativity matters.

47
00:06:49,991 --> 00:06:57,863
Parse 8 minus 6 minus 2. One tree groups 6 minus 2 first. This is the right associative parse.

48
00:06:58,313 --> 00:07:03,774
The other tree groups 8 minus 6 first. This is the left associative parse.

49
00:07:04,224 --> 00:07:11,840
The right associative tree gives 4. That is wrong. The left associative tree gives 0. That is correct.

50
00:07:12,290 --> 00:07:26,178
Subtraction is left associative. x minus y minus z means x minus y, then minus z. The problem is E goes to E minus E. E can grow on either side of the minus.

51
00:07:26,628 --> 00:07:48,217
The fix. Change E goes to E minus E into E goes to E minus F. The rule is left recursive: E appears only on the left of the minus. Division is also left associative, so F goes to F divided by an integer. Here is the leftmost derivation of 8 minus 6 minus 2.

52
00:07:48,667 --> 00:07:50,033
S becomes E.

53
00:07:50,483 --> 00:07:55,176
E becomes E minus F. The orange symbol is the one we expand.

54
00:07:55,626 --> 00:08:01,450
Expand the left E again: E minus F. Now we have E minus F minus F.

55
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:06,508
The last E becomes F, because the only other choice adds a minus.

56
00:08:06,958 --> 00:08:11,758
The first F becomes 8. We always expand the leftmost nonterminal.

57
00:08:12,208 --> 00:08:13,936
The second F becomes 6.

58
00:08:14,386 --> 00:08:25,757
The last F becomes 2. The tree leans to the left. 8 minus 6 is done first, then minus 2. The value is 0, and a check found exactly one tree.

59
00:08:26,207 --> 00:08:34,847
Exponentiation is the opposite. It is right associative. x to the y to the z means x to the power y to the z.

60
00:08:35,297 --> 00:08:54,177
So the rule is right recursive: E goes to F, caret, E. Only the right side can grow. For 2 caret 3 caret 2, the tree leans right. 3 squared is done first, and the value is 2 to the 9, which is 512. The other grouping would give 64.

61
00:08:54,627 --> 00:09:03,992
The pattern: a left associative operator needs a left recursive rule. A right associative operator needs a right recursive rule.

62
00:09:04,442 --> 00:09:14,917
Now a problem. Left associativity needs left recursion. But an L L one grammar cannot have left recursion. Video 19 shows why.

63
00:09:15,367 --> 00:09:34,481
We can remove the left recursion with a tail rule: E goes to F T, and T goes to minus F T, or epsilon. This grammar is L L one. But look at the tree. Now the tree leans to the right. Removing the left recursion also changed the associativity.

64
00:09:34,931 --> 00:09:51,913
The notes say there is no simple solution in B N F. But in E B N F we can use repetition: an Expr is a Term, followed by zero or more minus Term. The grammar gives a flat list. It does not fix the associativity.

65
00:09:52,363 --> 00:10:07,979
Then the parser implementation combines the list from the left. 8 minus 6 is 2, and 2 minus 2 is 0. This E B N F grammar is weakly equivalent: it accepts the same strings, but it builds different trees.

66
00:10:08,429 --> 00:10:25,581
Now a classic ambiguity: the dangling else. This line gives the enrollment rules for E C E 351. If the department is E C E, if the term is 3 A, enroll is yes. Else, enroll needs an override.

67
00:10:26,031 --> 00:10:35,396
Here is the typical grammar for an optional else. A conditional is if, expression, statement. Or if, expression, statement, else, statement.

68
00:10:35,846 --> 00:10:45,638
Third year E C E students can enroll. But who needs an override? It depends on which if owns the else, the inner if or the outer if.

69
00:10:46,088 --> 00:10:55,453
This problem comes from Algol 60. Algol 60 gave us the B N F notation, and C is an industrial descendant of it.

70
00:10:55,903 --> 00:11:00,853
Here are the two parse trees. The leaves read the same line from left to right.

71
00:11:01,303 --> 00:11:06,103
In tree 1, the inner conditional has the else. The outer if has no else.

72
00:11:06,553 --> 00:11:15,193
In tree 2, the outer conditional has the else, and the inner if has none. Both trees accept. So the grammar is ambiguous.

73
00:11:15,643 --> 00:11:27,717
The two trees are two different programs. With the else on the inner if, E C E students in a different term use an override. Students outside E C E get nothing.

74
00:11:28,167 --> 00:11:41,138
With the else on the outer if, students from outside E C E use an override. E C E students in other terms get nothing. The same text gives two different enrollment rules.

75
00:11:41,588 --> 00:11:50,292
In practice there are four ways to resolve the dangling else. One: every if needs an end if. V H D L does this.

76
00:11:50,742 --> 00:11:53,899
Two: every if needs an else. Haskell does this.

77
00:11:54,349 --> 00:11:57,869
Three: put indentation into the grammar. Python does this.

78
00:11:58,319 --> 00:12:06,746
Four: add a note to the language specification. C and Java do this. The note says that the else goes with the inner if.

79
00:12:07,196 --> 00:12:16,177
Exercise 4.6.1 from the notes. Which of the four approaches change the grammar, so that the ambiguity is gone?

80
00:12:16,627 --> 00:12:18,270
Pause the video and try it.

81
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:33,504
The answer is the first three. End if, a required else, and indentation all change the grammar. The note in the specification leaves the grammar ambiguous. The parser writer must deal with this case specially.

82
00:12:33,954 --> 00:12:45,623
Exercise 4.6.2. For the approaches that remove the ambiguity, write the new grammar. The notes do not give a solution yet, so this is our solution.

83
00:12:46,073 --> 00:12:47,716
Pause the video and try it.

84
00:12:48,166 --> 00:13:04,294
With end if, each conditional closes with end if. Now the programmer must write where the inner if ends. If end if comes after the else, the else is inside the inner if. If the end if comes first, the else belongs to the outer if.

85
00:13:04,744 --> 00:13:11,443
With a required else, there is only one form. Every if has its own else, so no else is left over.

86
00:13:11,893 --> 00:13:29,237
A python check counted the parse trees of every string up to 8 symbols. Both new grammars give at most one tree. The old grammar gives two trees for some strings. Indentation needs special tokens from the lexer, so we do not write that grammar here.

87
00:13:29,687 --> 00:13:50,636
Now exercises 4.11.6 and 4.11.7 from the notes. The grammar is E goes to E plus E, E times E, or int. First, give an input string with two parse trees. Second, refactor the grammar for normal precedence, and make it right associative.

88
00:13:51,086 --> 00:13:52,729
Pause the video and try it.

89
00:13:53,179 --> 00:14:01,115
Part 6. The string 1 plus 2 times 3 has two trees, as we saw. 1 plus 2 plus 3 also works.

90
00:14:01,565 --> 00:14:17,010
Part 7. Our solution follows the notes. E goes to F plus E, or F. F goes to int times F, or int. Plus is at the low level, and times is at the high level. Both rules are right recursive.

91
00:14:17,460 --> 00:14:22,089
1 plus 2 times 3 has one tree: 1 plus, 2 times 3.

92
00:14:22,539 --> 00:14:29,643
1 plus 2 plus 3 also has one tree. It leans right, so the grammar is right associative.

93
00:14:30,093 --> 00:14:44,408
The notes also show a wrong first try: E goes to E plus E or F, and F goes to F times F or int. The precedence is correct, but it is not right associative, and it is still ambiguous.

94
00:14:44,858 --> 00:15:02,863
A python check found at most one tree for every string up to length 7, and the same language as the original grammar. This grammar is not L L one, because of common prefixes. That is OK here. Video 19 shows an L L one version.

95
00:15:03,313 --> 00:15:24,113
Exercise 4.11.4 from the notes. A to E are binary operators. Each has a different precedence, and none is associative. The input 3 C 4 B 1 A 2 D 6 B 7 E 5 gives this tree. List the operators from highest to lowest precedence.

96
00:15:24,563 --> 00:15:26,206
Pause the video and try it.

97
00:15:26,656 --> 00:15:35,723
An operator lower in the tree is done first, so it has higher precedence than its parent. C is under B. So C is higher than B.

98
00:15:36,173 --> 00:15:41,101
B is under A, and D is under A. So B and D are both higher than A.

99
00:15:41,551 --> 00:15:48,057
E is under D, so E is higher than D. The second B is under E, so B is higher than E.

100
00:15:48,507 --> 00:15:58,939
Chain them: C, then B, then E, then D, then A. A python check tried all 120 orders. Only C B E D A fits all five facts.

101
00:15:59,389 --> 00:16:09,843
Now the short questions in questions-pre, section 12.3. Question 12.3.4. How do we show that a grammar is ambiguous?

102
00:16:10,293 --> 00:16:14,197
Show one input string that has two parse trees in the grammar.

103
00:16:14,647 --> 00:16:34,977
Question 12.3.5. How do we show that a grammar is unambiguous? The posted answer: that is mathematically beyond this course. We say that we cannot find an input string with two parses. In this video, a python search over all short strings gave us extra confidence.

104
00:16:35,427 --> 00:16:50,894
Question 12.3.7. How many kinds of grammar equivalence did we see? Two. Weak equivalence: the grammars accept the same strings. Strong equivalence: same strings, and exactly the same parse trees.

105
00:16:51,344 --> 00:17:04,741
Question 12.3.8. When we refactor a grammar, it is usually weakly equivalent. The precedence grammar accepts the same strings as the ambiguous one, but it builds different trees.

106
00:17:05,191 --> 00:17:15,303
The other questions in section 12.3 use this table from the notes, Figure 4.2. Each class of grammar needs a kind of machine.

107
00:17:15,753 --> 00:17:26,676
Question 12.3.1. What machine recognizes an L L one language? A pushdown automaton. The table says a deterministic one.

108
00:17:27,126 --> 00:17:44,171
Question 12.3.2. What grammar class is needed for a to the n, b to the n? A context free grammar. L L one is also a correct answer, because S goes to a S b, or a b, is in the L L one subset.

109
00:17:44,621 --> 00:17:56,931
Question 12.3.3. a to the n, b to the n, c to the n needs a context sensitive grammar. Counting three things is outside the context free class.

110
00:17:57,381 --> 00:18:09,349
Question 12.3.6. What can a C F G express that a peg cannot? Ambiguity. Pegs do not permit ambiguity. We study pegs after the midterm.

111
00:18:09,799 --> 00:18:18,140
To summarize. A grammar is ambiguous when one legal string has two parse trees. One example string is the proof.

112
00:18:18,590 --> 00:18:29,619
For precedence, give each level its own nonterminal. The lowest level is at the top. Each level uses the next higher level. The highest level has the operands.

113
00:18:30,069 --> 00:18:39,947
For associativity, a left associative operator needs a left recursive rule, and a right associative operator needs a right recursive rule.

114
00:18:40,397 --> 00:18:49,847
Left recursion is not L L one. Removing it changes the trees. E B N F repetition gives a weakly equivalent grammar.

115
00:18:50,297 --> 00:19:04,036
For the dangling else, end if, a required else, and indentation remove the ambiguity from the grammar. A note in the specification does not. The next video tests if a grammar is L L one.
